\chapter{Lessons and the page}

The page is React and Tailwind. It holds the lesson text, the recognised name and
the gravity switch, and it does not hold any per-frame state. The scene, with the
physics world, the renderer and the rope tube, lives in one component, which
creates them when it mounts and disposes of them when it unmounts.

\section{Lesson record}

A lesson is data. It names the knot, the stage that teaches it, its web sources,
the invariants a recogniser must match, and the prompt the player reads. Stage
one has one record, the overhand knot, so the page has something to show. The
guided, sandbox and quiz modes arrive in later stages and read the same records.

The source is the Knot Atlas entry for 3\textsubscript{1}, which the research
chapter records as confirmed. The determinant is three, as the topology chapter
computes. The steps are written for this page and are not from a cited tying
guide, so stage six must replace them with cited ones.

@d Lessons: record and stub @{
export interface Invariants {
  determinant: number;
}

export interface LessonRecord {
  id: string;
  name: string;
  stage: number;
  sources: readonly string[];
  invariants: Invariants;
  prompt: string;
  steps: readonly string[];
}

export const STAGE_ONE_LESSONS: readonly LessonRecord[] = [
  {
    id: 'overhand',
    name: 'Overhand knot',
    stage: 1,
    sources: ['https://katlas.org/wiki/3_1'],
    invariants: { determinant: 3 },
    prompt: 'Tie an overhand knot in the rope. The recogniser names it when it is tied.',
    steps: [
      'Press the mouse on a part of the rope and hold the button. The rope follows the mouse while the button is down.',
      'Scroll the wheel up to bring the held part closer to you, and down to push it farther away. The point stays under the mouse.',
      'Move the held part to make a loop, so that one part of the rope crosses in front of another.',
      'Grab one end and pass it over the rope, then under it.',
      'Grab the same end and pass it through the loop you made, so it comes out on the side it started from.',
      'Release the button and let the rope settle. The name appears at the bottom left. Gravity is off by default, so the rope floats; switch it on to let the rope fall.',
    ],
  },
];
@}

\section{Scene objects}

The bar and the floor are scene data: boxes with a centre and half sizes. The
rope model receives them as plain shapes, and the renderer draws them as meshes.
Neither owns them. The bar lies along the depth axis, above the rope, so a rope
dragged up to it can drape over it, and it cannot pass through it. The floor is
where the rope falls when gravity is on.

@d Anchors: bar @{
import type { Box } from '../rope/snapshot';

export const BAR: Box = {
  center: { x: 0.6, y: 0.45, z: 0 },
  half: { x: 0.02, y: 0.02, z: 0.4 },
};
@}

\section{Rope stage}

The stage component owns the whole scene. Four things keep it honest.

The physics loop runs only while the rope moves. When the fastest link is slower
than the settle speed for thirty frames, the loop stops, and a pointer event, a
wheel turn or a gravity change starts it again. That is render on demand, as the
architecture chapter asks.

Recognition runs at most four times a second while the rope moves, and once when
it settles. It sets state only when the name changes, so React re-renders on a
change of result, never on each frame.

Depth is the distance from the camera measured straight ahead, along the view
direction. A click grabs the nearest link under the pointer, within a few pixels,
and records that link's depth. While the button is held, the held point is where
the mouse ray meets the plane at that depth. It stays under the cursor, and it
keeps its depth as the cursor moves. The wheel changes the depth: up brings the
point closer to the camera, down pushes it farther. Measuring the distance along
the ray instead would make the point drift into the scene as the cursor moved
away from the middle of the view.

Gravity is a switch in the page. The page keeps the choice in a ref, so the scene
can read it when it finishes loading, and it updates the rope when the switch is
pressed.

The camera orbits when the player drags on empty space, and the wheel zooms it.
Over a link, the same actions move the link instead, so the camera is locked while
a link is held. The reset button returns the camera to its start view. Orbiting
changes only what the player sees. Recognition reads the rope along the same
fixed axis whatever the camera does, so the answer does not change with the view.

@d UI: rope stage @{
import { useEffect, useRef, useState } from 'react';
import * as THREE from 'three';
import { OrbitControls } from 'three/addons/controls/OrbitControls.js';
import { RopeTube } from '../render/ropeTube';
import { BAR } from '../anchors/bar';
import { LINK_COUNT, LINK_RADIUS, createRope, initPhysics, type Rope } from '../rope/rope';
import type { Box } from '../rope/snapshot';
import { recognise } from '../topology/recogniser';

const FLOOR: Box = { center: { x: 0, y: -1, z: 0 }, half: { x: 1.5, y: 0.05, z: 1.5 } };
const ROPE_START = { x: -0.975, y: 0.2, z: 0 };
const SETTLE_SPEED = 0.01;
const SETTLE_FRAMES = 30;
const RECOGNISE_EVERY_MS = 250;
const DEPTH_STEP = 0.0003;
const NEAR = 0.3;
const FAR = 6;
const PICK_RADIUS = 36;

export function RopeStage() {
  const hostRef = useRef<HTMLDivElement>(null);
  const gravityRef = useRef(false);
  const controls = useRef<{ setGravity(on: boolean): void } | null>(null);
  const view = useRef<{ reset(): void } | null>(null);
  const [name, setName] = useState<string | null>(null);
  const [gravityOn, setGravityOn] = useState(false);

  const resetView = () => {
    view.current?.reset();
  };

  const toggleGravity = () => {
    gravityRef.current = !gravityRef.current;
    setGravityOn(gravityRef.current);
    controls.current?.setGravity(gravityRef.current);
  };

  useEffect(() => {
    const host = hostRef.current;
    if (!host) return;

    let disposed = false;
    let frame = 0;
    let running = false;
    let still = 0;
    let lastRecognised = 0;
    let cleanup = () => {};
    const owned: { dispose(): void }[] = [];

    const raycaster = new THREE.Raycaster();
    const pointer = new THREE.Vector2();
    const hit = new THREE.Vector3();
    const screen = new THREE.Vector3();
    const facing = new THREE.Vector3();
    let grabbing = false;
    let depth = 0;

    initPhysics().then(() => {
      if (disposed) return;

      const rope: Rope = createRope(ROPE_START, [BAR, FLOOR]);
      rope.setGravity(gravityRef.current);
      const tube = new RopeTube(LINK_COUNT, LINK_RADIUS);
      owned.push(rope, tube);

      const scene = new THREE.Scene();
      scene.background = new THREE.Color(0x0f172a);
      scene.add(tube.mesh);
      scene.add(new THREE.AmbientLight(0xffffff, 0.6));
      const light = new THREE.DirectionalLight(0xffffff, 1.2);
      light.position.set(1, 2, 3);
      scene.add(light);

      const addBox = (box: Box, color: number) => {
        const geometry = new THREE.BoxGeometry(box.half.x * 2, box.half.y * 2, box.half.z * 2);
        const material = new THREE.MeshStandardMaterial({ color });
        const mesh = new THREE.Mesh(geometry, material);
        mesh.position.set(box.center.x, box.center.y, box.center.z);
        scene.add(mesh);
        owned.push(geometry, material);
      };
      addBox(BAR, 0x64748b);
      addBox(FLOOR, 0x1e293b);

      const camera = new THREE.PerspectiveCamera(40, host.clientWidth / host.clientHeight, 0.01, 100);
      camera.position.set(1.1, 0.9, 3.4);
      camera.lookAt(0.1, 0.1, 0);

      const renderer = new THREE.WebGLRenderer({ antialias: true });
      renderer.setPixelRatio(Math.min(window.devicePixelRatio, 2));
      renderer.setSize(host.clientWidth, host.clientHeight);
      host.appendChild(renderer.domElement);

      const canvas = renderer.domElement;

      // Empty space orbits the camera, and the wheel zooms. A held link turns both off.
      const orbit = new OrbitControls(camera, canvas);
      orbit.enableDamping = true;
      orbit.minDistance = 1;
      orbit.maxDistance = 8;
      orbit.target.set(0.1, 0.1, 0);
      orbit.update();
      orbit.saveState();
      owned.push(orbit);
      view.current = {
        reset() {
          orbit.reset();
          wake();
        },
      };

      controls.current = {
        setGravity(on) {
          rope.setGravity(on);
          wake();
        },
      };

      const animate = () => {
        orbit.update();
        const fastest = rope.step();
        const snapshot = rope.snapshot();
        tube.update(snapshot);
        renderer.render(scene, camera);

        still = fastest < SETTLE_SPEED ? still + 1 : 0;
        const now = performance.now();
        if (still === SETTLE_FRAMES || (still === 0 && now - lastRecognised > RECOGNISE_EVERY_MS)) {
          lastRecognised = now;
          setName(recognise(snapshot).name);
        }
        if (still < SETTLE_FRAMES) {
          frame = requestAnimationFrame(animate);
        } else {
          running = false;
        }
      };

      const wake = () => {
        still = 0;
        if (!running) {
          running = true;
          frame = requestAnimationFrame(animate);
        }
      };

      // Orbiting, damping and zoom redraw the scene, so they wake the loop.
      orbit.addEventListener('change', wake);

      const pointerRay = (event: PointerEvent) => {
        const rect = canvas.getBoundingClientRect();
        pointer.set(
          ((event.clientX - rect.left) / rect.width) * 2 - 1,
          -((event.clientY - rect.top) / rect.height) * 2 + 1,
        );
        raycaster.setFromCamera(pointer, camera);
      };

      // The nearest link whose on-screen position is within PICK_RADIUS pixels, or null.
      const pickLink = (event: PointerEvent): number | null => {
        const rect = canvas.getBoundingClientRect();
        const points = rope.snapshot().points;
        let best: number | null = null;
        let bestDistance = PICK_RADIUS;
        for (let i = 0; i < points.length; i++) {
          const p = points[i];
          screen.set(p.x, p.y, p.z).project(camera);
          const x = rect.left + ((screen.x + 1) / 2) * rect.width;
          const y = rect.top + ((1 - screen.y) / 2) * rect.height;
          const distance = Math.hypot(x - event.clientX, y - event.clientY);
          if (distance < bestDistance) {
            bestDistance = distance;
            best = i;
          }
        }
        return best;
      };

      // The point on the mouse ray that lies the given distance ahead of the camera, along the view direction.
      const pointAtDepth = (distance: number): THREE.Vector3 => {
        camera.getWorldDirection(facing);
        const along = raycaster.ray.direction.dot(facing);
        return raycaster.ray.at(distance / along, hit);
      };

      const onDown = (event: PointerEvent) => {
        const index = pickLink(event);
        if (index === null) return;
        pointerRay(event);
        const at = rope.snapshot().points[index];
        // The held point keeps this depth until the wheel changes it.
        camera.getWorldDirection(facing);
        depth = hit.set(at.x, at.y, at.z).sub(camera.position).dot(facing);
        grabbing = true;
        orbit.enabled = false;
        canvas.setPointerCapture(event.pointerId);
        rope.grabLink(index, at);
        wake();
      };
      const onMove = (event: PointerEvent) => {
        if (!grabbing) return;
        pointerRay(event);
        rope.moveGrab(pointAtDepth(depth));
        wake();
      };
      const onUp = () => {
        if (!grabbing) return;
        grabbing = false;
        orbit.enabled = true;
        rope.releaseGrab();
        wake();
      };
      const onWheel = (event: WheelEvent) => {
        if (!grabbing) return;
        // The ray is the last pointer ray, so the held point stays under the cursor.
        depth = THREE.MathUtils.clamp(depth + event.deltaY * DEPTH_STEP, NEAR, FAR);
        rope.moveGrab(pointAtDepth(depth));
        wake();
      };
      const onResize = () => {
        camera.aspect = host.clientWidth / host.clientHeight;
        camera.updateProjectionMatrix();
        renderer.setSize(host.clientWidth, host.clientHeight);
      };

      // Capture phase, so a click on a link is taken before the orbit controls see it.
      canvas.addEventListener('pointerdown', onDown, { capture: true });
      canvas.addEventListener('pointermove', onMove);
      canvas.addEventListener('pointerup', onUp);
      canvas.addEventListener('pointercancel', onUp);
      canvas.addEventListener('wheel', onWheel);
      window.addEventListener('resize', onResize);

      cleanup = () => {
        canvas.removeEventListener('pointerdown', onDown, { capture: true });
        canvas.removeEventListener('pointermove', onMove);
        canvas.removeEventListener('pointerup', onUp);
        canvas.removeEventListener('pointercancel', onUp);
        canvas.removeEventListener('wheel', onWheel);
        window.removeEventListener('resize', onResize);
        renderer.dispose();
        renderer.domElement.remove();
      };

      wake();
    });

    return () => {
      disposed = true;
      cancelAnimationFrame(frame);
      cleanup();
      controls.current = null;
      for (const item of owned) item.dispose();
    };
  }, []);

  return (
    <div className="relative h-full w-full">
      <div ref={hostRef} className="absolute inset-0" />
      <p className="pointer-events-none absolute bottom-3 left-3 rounded bg-slate-900/80 px-2 py-1 text-sm text-slate-200">
        Recognised: {name ?? 'nothing yet'}
      </p>
      <div className="absolute bottom-3 right-3 flex gap-2">
        <button
          type="button"
          onClick={resetView}
          className="rounded bg-slate-900/80 px-3 py-1 text-sm text-slate-100 hover:bg-slate-800"
        >
          Reset view
        </button>
        <button
          type="button"
          onClick={toggleGravity}
          className="rounded bg-slate-900/80 px-3 py-1 text-sm text-slate-100 hover:bg-slate-800"
        >
          Gravity: {gravityOn ? 'on' : 'off'}
        </button>
      </div>
    </div>
  );
}
@}

\section{Application}

The application is one screen: the lesson's name, its prompt and its numbered
steps at the top, the rope stage filling the rest. The steps are what the player
follows, so a lesson cannot be used without them. The stage is the only part with
per-frame work.

@d UI: application @{
import { STAGE_ONE_LESSONS } from './lessons/record';
import { RopeStage } from './ui/RopeStage';

export function App() {
  const lesson = STAGE_ONE_LESSONS[0];
  return (
    <main className="flex h-full flex-col bg-slate-950 text-slate-100">
      <header className="border-b border-slate-800 px-4 py-3">
        <h1 className="text-lg font-semibold">Roppi</h1>
        <p className="text-sm text-slate-400">
          {lesson.name}. {lesson.prompt}
        </p>
        <ol className="mt-2 list-decimal space-y-0.5 pl-5 text-sm text-slate-300">
          {lesson.steps.map((step) => (
            <li key={step}>{step}</li>
          ))}
        </ol>
      </header>
      <section className="relative min-h-0 flex-1">
        <RopeStage />
      </section>
    </main>
  );
}
@}
